- "name": the {{ kind }}'s name.{% if has_name %} Use the name already decided.{% else %} Invent one if the prompt doesn't give it.{% endif %}
{% if kind == "character" -%}
- "age": how old the character is now, in years, as a number.
{% endif -%}
- "facts": between {{ min_facts }} and {{ max_facts }} facts, one claim each. "category" is one of: {{ categories | join(", ") }}.
- "since" is optional. Give it only when a fact started at a known time, and only as a year of this calendar, as a bare number no later than {{ now_year }}; "YEAR" in the example stands for that number. Leave it out otherwise: never a phrase like "childhood" or "ten years ago".
- Leave "since" out for an inherent trait that has always been true -- a species, the appearance someone is born with, a lifelong temperament -- rather than dating it to the present year.
{%- endif %}
- "aka": other names for this {{ kind }} itself, if it has any, each {"name", "tag", "everyday"}. "tag" is "proper" for another formal name, or "common" for what people in the world actually call it day to day (a short form, a nickname). Set "everyday" true on the one common name people use most, false on the rest. "name" stays the most formal name. An empty list is fine.
- "names": after the facts, every proper name your facts use, other than this {{ kind }}'s own: people, places, peoples and species, named events, named groups and named objects, new or already in the world. Each is {"name", "kind", "tag", "in", "aka"}: "name" in full, as your facts write it; "kind" one of character, place, species, race, event, item, lore (a country, realm or city is a place; a faith, order, council, guild, culture, field of knowledge or magic, law, script or language is lore); "tag" what it is, as for the {{ kind }} (never "proper" or "common": those are only for other names); "in" the name of the entry it belongs in (this {{ kind }}, an entry listed above, or another of your names), or null; "aka" any other names your facts use for the same thing. Proper names only: never a description such as "the postmaster", "a war" or "his mother", nor a title on its own such as "the Tsar" or "the High Bishop", nor a people's adjective such as "Moscavian" on its own -- but "Yelena" in "his mother Yelena" is a name. An empty list is fine.
- Only named things become entries. A role, rank or office (a harbour master, the keeper of the seals), a topic (grain tariffs, winter trade) or a practice without a proper name of its own (the evening bell, night sessions) is never one of the "names": it stays a fact of the {{ kind }}.
{%- if nearby %}
- Keep "facts" to claims mainly about the {{ kind }} itself: a claim mainly about another entry goes in "elsewhere".
- "elsewhere": after the names, what you would add about other entries, each {"entry", "text", "held_by"}: "entry" the entry's name as the list of entries near this one writes it, or a new name you also list in "names"; "text" one claim in a full sentence. Never the {{ kind }} itself. An empty list is fine.
- A claim that ties another entry to the {{ kind }} ("it is taught at X", "unlike X, it...", "X uses it") is the {{ kind }}'s own: it goes in "facts". "elsewhere" is only for what another entry's page should say even if the {{ kind }} didn't exist.
{%- endif %}
{%- if nearby %}
- "tag": one or two words for what the {{ kind }} is, such as river, ruler, custom, institution, regiment, capital or doctrine. It says what the {{ kind }} itself is, not what its parts or members are. Use a tag from the tags in use when one fits; make a new one only when none does.
- "in": the entries listed above that the {{ kind }} belongs in, each {"entry", "role"}: "entry" the entry's name as the list writes it; "role" a few words for how it belongs there ("flows through", "sovereign", "practitioner", "branch of", "taught here"), or null. A thing can be in several entries: a road in each realm it crosses, a temple in its city and in the faith it serves. Look past places: a thing also belongs in the field, faith, order or council it is part of, and in the people or culture it comes from. Never one of the {{ kind }}'s own parts: those go in "file_into". Only entries from the list; an empty list is fine.
- "file_into": the entries listed above that belong in this {{ kind }}: its parts, members, branches or what it holds (an entry the list or its facts call a part, unit, branch or member of this {{ kind }} belongs here), each {"entry", "role"}, "role" how that entry belongs in this {{ kind }}, or null. Never an entry you put in "in". Only entries from the list; an empty list is fine.
{%- endif %}
{%- if "belief" in categories %}
- "belief": every fact that says what the {{ kind }} teaches, holds or believes ("it teaches that...", "the faithful hold that...", "its doctrine says...") is a "belief", never "general". Plain truth of the world -- its rites as practised, its clergy, places, ranks and history -- goes in the other categories.
{%- endif %}
{%- if spheres %}
- "ties": after the names, the spheres above this character belongs to, each {"sphere", "role", "since", "exceptions"}: "sphere" its name as written above; "role" a word or two for how they belong (practitioner, priest, elder, scholar, member); "since" the year they joined, as a bare number no later than {{ now_year }}, or left out. Only the spheres above: never invent one here. An empty list is fine.
- "exceptions": only where this character departs from the sphere, each {"text", "rejects"}: "text" one sentence on what they reject or do differently, and "rejects" the label of the item it departs from, such as "S3". The character already holds everything else the sphere holds, so never restate it, in an exception or in the facts. Most ties have no exception or one.
- Write each exception only there, never again as one of the facts.
{%- endif %}

No commentary, no markdown fences, just the JSON object.
<|CLOSE_SECTION|>
